\section{Getting started}
\label{tut:getting-started}

This tutorial gives a student's first end-to-end pass through
[[learnlog]]: initialise a project, activate the project-local
environment, confirm that the environment records runs, run a program,
and inspect the recorded commit in batch mode.
The object of learning is this basic workflow as a connected whole, so
the tutorial contrasts an ordinary directory with an initialised
learnlog project and delays the explanation of playback until the
learner has first produced a run to inspect.
The critical aspects the steps make discernible are that
[[learnlog init]] anchors the project at the root,
[[learnlog activate]] selects the project [[.venv/]], and
[[learnlog play -b]] reveals the resulting commit history.
Together the steps follow a separation pattern: the project stays
invariant while each step rotates one aspect of the workflow into
focus---anchoring, interpreter selection, wiring, writing,
running---and the final playback step fuses them by showing every
earlier aspect in one recorded run.

\subsection{Two variants, one contract}

The tutorial tangles into two catalog entries.
[[getting-started]] addresses novices who have never programmed
before; [[getting-started-expert]] addresses experienced programmers.
Both practise the same workflow, and every step keeps the same title,
the same commands, and the same machine-checked completion patterns;
what varies between the variants is whether the mechanism behind each
step is visible.
For the expert the mechanism is part of the object of learning---the
fresh shell each step opens, the per-shell activation, the [[.pth]]
autostart hook, the Git record---while for the novice it is
deliberately backgrounded: a learner without a working model of shells
and interpreters has nothing to attach such explanations to, so they
cost attention without adding discernment.
The clearest example is the runner's re-activation: every step opens a
fresh shell in which the runner replays [[eval "$(learnlog activate)"]]
before handing over.
The expert variant explains this; the novice variant simply never
mentions it.

The sharing is enforced by construction rather than by discipline:
each step's checks are defined once, in a chunk both variants
reference, so the tangled tutorials can drift apart only in their
prose and hints---never in what they ask the learner to do.

