\subsection{Create a fresh project}

The step's object of learning is that a project is anchored at a
directory root: [[learnlog init]] turns the directory it runs in, and
everything below it, into the recorded project.
The contrast that carries this is an ordinary directory against an
initialised one, in which [[.learnlog/]] and [[.venv/]] appear.
The home-directory refusal is stated as an expectation on \emph{where}
to run [[init]] rather than demonstrated: making the learner trip the
guard would teach [[--force]] as a step in the workflow, when it is
the exception to it.
Both variants share the checks and the hint; the prose diverges on
that refusal---the novice hears what would go wrong in terms of their
own files, the expert in terms of what would be committed, and only
the expert meets [[--force]] and learns what the two directories
really are.

<<shared step: create a fresh project>>=
# Create a fresh project

```tutorial-step
required_patterns:
  - learnlog init python
hint: Run `learnlog init python` in an empty project directory.
```
<<novice step: create a fresh project>>=
<<shared step: create a fresh project>>

Start in a new directory made just for this practice project and
initialise it with `learnlog init python`.
Use a directory of its own, not your home directory: `learnlog init`
refuses to turn your home directory into a project, because then it
would record everything you keep there instead of just this project.

This creates a hidden folder called `.learnlog/`, where your runs will
be saved, and a `.venv/` folder with the project's own Python.
<<expert step: create a fresh project>>=
<<shared step: create a fresh project>>

Start in a new directory for a throwaway practice project and initialise
it with `learnlog init python`.
Make it a directory of its own, not your home directory: `learnlog init`
refuses to make `~` (or anything above it) a project root, because every
later run would then commit unrelated files, from `.ssh/` to your
browser cache, into the log.
The refusal names a `--force` escape hatch for the rare case where you
really mean it; you do not need it here.

This creates the hidden `.learnlog/` repository---a Git repository
whose work tree is your project directory---and prepares a local
`.venv/` for the project, wired so that its Python records every run.
@

\subsection{Activate the project environment}

Recording belongs to the project's own interpreter, and this step's
object of learning is that activation is how the learner selects it.
The step then generalises \emph{who} performs the selection: the
learner in a plain shell, or an editor---VS Code and PyCharm detect
the project [[.venv/]] and activate it on their own.
The agent varies while the selected interpreter stays invariant,
which carries the practical corollary that in an automatically
activating editor, [[learnlog init python]] once is all the setup a
learner needs.
The corollary rests on recording following the interpreter rather
than the activation---a run of the venv's Python from an unactivated
shell is recorded---so the expert variant states the mechanism
outright, while the novice variant gives only the rule and points at
the next step's [[learnlog doctor]] as the way to confirm what an
editor selected.
The instruction is identical for both audiences, so the step's core
is shared; the expert variant appends why it works---per-shell
activation against per-venv wiring---which the later steps'
re-activations then build on.

<<shared step: activate the project environment>>=
# Activate the project environment

```tutorial-step
required_patterns:
  - learnlog activate
hint: Use `eval "$(learnlog activate)"` to enter the project `.venv/`.
```

Run `eval "$(learnlog activate)"` so that the project's own Python and
packages are the ones you use in the next steps.

You need this command in a plain terminal, like the one this tutorial
runs in.
If you instead open the project folder in an editor such as VS Code or
PyCharm, the editor finds the project's own Python and uses it
automatically---then initialising once is all you need, and the next
step shows you how to confirm that.
<<novice step: activate the project environment>>=
<<shared step: activate the project environment>>
<<expert step: activate the project environment>>=
<<shared step: activate the project environment>>

Activation is per shell: the command prints shell code that puts the
project `.venv/` first, and the `eval` applies it to the shell you are
in.
The recording wiring itself lives in the venv, not in the shell, so
any shell that activates this venv runs a recording Python.
In fact, recording follows the interpreter rather than the
activation: even `./.venv/bin/python hello.py` from an unactivated
shell is recorded.
That is why the editors work without further setup---VS Code and
PyCharm detect the workspace `.venv/`, select it as the project
interpreter, and by default activate it in their integrated
terminals, so both their Run buttons and their terminals use the
recording Python.
If you are unsure what your editor selected, the next step's
`learnlog doctor` answers for exactly the interpreter it runs with.
@

\subsection{Confirm that runs are being recorded}

The step's object of learning is that recording status is an
inspectable property: the learner runs [[learnlog doctor]] and reads
the answer from the tool before the text names the possible states.
Reading the answer to \enquote{are my runs recorded?} from the tool,
rather than being told, is what makes the invisible wiring an object
the learner can inspect later on their own.
The checks are shared through a common chunk, but hint and prose
diverge: the report is a property of one interpreter, which only means
something to a reader who knows what an interpreter is.
The novice variant compresses the report to the one question it
answers; the expert variant keeps the three states, the per-interpreter
framing---and with it the runner's re-activation---and the [[.pth]]
autostart hook behind the wiring.

<<checks: confirm that runs are being recorded>>=
pre_command: |
  eval "$(learnlog activate)"
required_patterns:
  - learnlog doctor
  - Runs of this interpreter are recorded in
<<novice step: confirm that runs are being recorded>>=
# Confirm that runs are being recorded

```tutorial-step
<<checks: confirm that runs are being recorded>>
hint: Run `learnlog doctor` and read where it says runs are recorded.
```

Run `learnlog doctor` and read what it prints before reading on.

It answers one question: will your runs be recorded?
Either it says `Runs of this interpreter are recorded in ...` and
names where, or it tells you that recording is not set up here and
what to run to fix it.
Only the first answer means your next run will be recorded.
<<expert step: confirm that runs are being recorded>>=
# Confirm that runs are being recorded

```tutorial-step
<<checks: confirm that runs are being recorded>>
hint: The tutorial already re-activated the project environment; run `learnlog doctor` and read where it says runs are recorded.
```

Run `learnlog doctor` and read what it prints before reading on.

The report is about one Python interpreter, which is why the tutorial
re-activates the project environment in the fresh shell this step
opens: the answer only means something for the Python you are about to
run your programs with.
It says one of three things.
Either the environment is wired and `Runs of this interpreter are
recorded in ...` names the log it writes to, or it is not wired at all,
or it is wired to a project that is no longer there.
Only the first means your next run will be recorded.
In the other two the last line names the fix: `learnlog init python` for
an environment nobody wired, and `learnlog doctor --repair`, run from
inside the project directory, for one whose project has gone.

The wiring the report checks is a `.pth` autostart hook inside the
venv: the interpreter imports learnlog at startup, before your code,
which is how even `python -c` one-liners and syntax errors end up
recorded.
@

\subsection{Create a small program}

The step's object of learning is transparency: the learner's normal
tools and habits are unchanged---any editor, a plain file.
The tutorial hands over to a real terminal here, so the step has a
[[pre_command]] but no completion patterns; both variants share the
whole check block.
The novice gets one concrete editor to fall back on; the expert gets
the choice spelled out and is told about the re-activated terminal,
which is exactly the mechanism sentence that would lose a novice.

<<shared step: create a small program>>=
# Create a small program

```tutorial-step
pre_command: |
  eval "$(learnlog activate)"
hint: Create `hello.py` in the terminal with your preferred editor, then return.
```
<<novice step: create a small program>>=
<<shared step: create a small program>>

Create a file named `hello.py` that prints a short message.
The tutorial drops you into a terminal so you can use an editor; for
example, run `nano hello.py`.
Type `print("Hello, world!")`, save the file, leave the editor, and
return to the tutorial.
<<expert step: create a small program>>=
<<shared step: create a small program>>

Create a file named `hello.py` that prints a short message.
In this step the tutorial should drop to a terminal so you can run
your preferred editor yourself.
The tutorial re-activates the project environment before opening this
terminal, so extra commands you try here still use the project's own
Python.
For example, you might run `code hello.py`, `vim hello.py`, or
`nano hello.py`.
Save `print("Hello, world!")` before returning.
@

\subsection{Run the program}

The step's object of learning is that capture is automatic on an
ordinary run: the contrast is between what the learner types---nothing
beyond [[python hello.py]]---and what gets recorded.
The checks accept equally valid solutions ([[python3]], a leading
[[./]], case and punctuation variation in the greeting) so the
contrast is not muddied by rejecting a correct run.
The novice variant states the outcome and stops; the expert variant
points at the dog that did not bark---no import, no wrapper---and
names the autostart hook as the reason.

<<checks: run the program>>=
pre_command: |
  eval "$(learnlog activate)"
required_patterns:
  - mode: regex
    pattern: 'python3?\s+(?:\./)?hello\.py'
  - mode: regex
    pattern: '(?i)hello[,!\s]*world'
<<novice step: run the program>>=
# Run the program

```tutorial-step
<<checks: run the program>>
hint: Run `python hello.py`.
```

Run `python hello.py`.
You should see your message on the screen.
That is all: learnlog records the run without you typing anything
extra.
<<expert step: run the program>>=
# Run the program

```tutorial-step
<<checks: run the program>>
hint: The tutorial already re-activated the project environment; run `python hello.py`.
```

This step also starts in a fresh shell, and the tutorial has already
re-activated the project environment for you.
Run `python hello.py`.
You should see your message on screen, and learnlog should record the
run automatically.
Note what you did not type: no import and no wrapper command.
The venv's autostart hook imported learnlog before your script ran, so
an ordinary invocation is enough.
@

\subsection{Inspect the recorded run}

The final step fuses the aspects the earlier steps separated: the
project, the interpreter, the wiring, and the run all become visible
in one record that [[learnlog play -b]] prints.
Its object of learning is that the run became an inspectable record
joining code state, command, and output.
The checks match the learner's own artefacts (the file name, the
greeting, a rendered diff) rather than the viewer's output format, so
the tutorial does not break when the viewer's formatting changes.
The novice variant asks the learner to recognise their own work in the
record; the expert variant names the viewer's field labels and closes
with the two-commit protocol, which explains a property the learner
can only appreciate here: even a crashed run leaves a record.

<<checks: inspect the recorded run>>=
pre_command: |
  eval "$(learnlog activate)"
required_patterns:
  - mode: regex
    pattern: 'learnlog\s+play\b[^\n]*(?:-b\b|--batch\b)'
  - hello.py
  - mode: regex
    pattern: '(?i)hello[,!\s]*world'
  - diff --git
<<novice step: inspect the recorded run>>=
# Inspect the recorded run

```tutorial-step
<<checks: inspect the recorded run>>
hint: Use `learnlog play -b` and look for your `hello.py` run.
```

Use `learnlog play -b` to print the recorded run in the terminal.
You should recognise what you just did: the name `hello.py`, the
message it printed, and the line you wrote.
<<expert step: inspect the recorded run>>=
# Inspect the recorded run

```tutorial-step
<<checks: inspect the recorded run>>
hint: The tutorial already re-activated the project environment; use `learnlog play -b` and confirm the recorded `hello.py` run appears.
```

This step also opens in a fresh shell, and the tutorial has already
re-activated the project environment for you.
Use `learnlog play -b` to print the recorded commit to the terminal.
Look for `Script: hello.py`, `stdout: Hello, world!`, and the diff
showing the file you created.

Under the hood the run is a pair of Git commits in `.learnlog/`: a
header committed before your program ran, holding the code state, and
a trailer committed afterwards, holding the results, joined by a run
id.
That is why a run that crashes---or is killed---still leaves a record,
and why `play -b` can show the pair merged as one entry.
@
